WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.156
This is Play the Recorder from Zero, by FlautaQuest. The soprano recorder, one step at a time.

00:00:07.956 --> 00:00:11.223
Today: meet the recorder and posture and hands.

00:00:13.023 --> 00:00:14.144
Before you start.

00:00:14.944 --> 00:00:32.421
The recorder is one of the friendliest instruments there is. It is small, light and affordable, and it makes a real musical sound from the very first day: no reed to wet, no strings to tune. That is why so many people, children and adults, start their musical life with one.

00:00:33.221 --> 00:00:45.858
The ebook takes you from zero to your first songs on the soprano recorder: how to hold it, how to breathe and use your tongue, the fingerings of the first notes, how to read simple music, and how to practise and look after your instrument.

00:00:47.358 --> 00:00:48.214
What you need.

00:00:49.014 --> 00:01:01.985
A soprano recorder (also called a descant recorder), ideally with baroque fingering. Chapter 1 explains what that means and how to check which kind you have. Plastic recorders are perfectly good for learning.

00:01:03.485 --> 00:01:05.415
How to read the fingering charts.

00:01:06.215 --> 00:01:17.233
The fingerings in the ebook are drawn as small charts. The thumb hole on the back is 0, the three holes for the left hand are 1 2 3, and the four for the right hand are 4 5 6 7.

00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:20.418
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:01:21.218 --> 00:01:31.302
An X means the hole is covered; an o means it is open. A slash, /, means the thumb hole is only partly covered. You will meet that later.

00:01:32.802 --> 00:01:34.199
How to use the ebook.

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:43.468
Read one chapter at a time and do its practice section with the recorder in your hands. A few short sessions a week are worth more than one long one.

00:01:44.268 --> 00:01:51.832
Tip: Play softly. The recorder needs much less air than you think, and almost every squeak comes from blowing too hard.

00:01:53.632 --> 00:01:58.066
Meet the recorder. Parts, range and fingering systems.

00:01:58.866 --> 00:02:07.030
Before you play, take a minute to get to know your instrument: what its parts are called, what notes it can play, and which fingering system it uses.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:09.273
The parts.

00:02:10.073 --> 00:02:13.373
Most soprano recorders come in two or three pieces:

00:02:14.173 --> 00:02:27.951
The head joint, at the top. You blow into the narrow slot at the end, the windway. The air goes through it and hits a sharp edge just below the small rectangular opening, the window. That edge is what makes the sound.

00:02:28.751 --> 00:02:33.109
The body, with the thumb hole on the back and most of the finger holes on the front.

00:02:33.909 --> 00:02:42.642
The foot joint, at the bottom, with the last hole. On three-piece recorders you can turn it a little so your little finger reaches the hole comfortably.

00:02:43.442 --> 00:02:49.604
Never cover the window or touch the edge. It is delicate, and a small dent can spoil the sound.

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:51.708
Range.

00:02:52.508 --> 00:03:05.869
The soprano recorder is in C. Its lowest note, with every hole covered, is the C an octave above middle C, called C5. Its normal range goes up to about D7, a little over two octaves.

00:03:06.669 --> 00:03:19.755
Music for soprano recorder is usually written an octave lower than it sounds. That keeps the notes on the staff, so the music is easier to read. You do not need to do anything about it: just read the notes as written.

00:03:21.255 --> 00:03:23.080
Baroque or German fingering?.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.043
There are two fingering systems for the soprano recorder:

00:03:27.843 --> 00:03:34.432
Baroque fingering, also called English fingering, is the one used by most teachers, and the one the ebook teaches.

00:03:35.232 --> 00:03:45.002
German fingering was developed in the early twentieth century. It makes the note F simpler to finger, but sharps and flats become harder to play and to play in tune.

00:03:45.802 --> 00:04:09.488
Many recorders have a letter near the thumb hole: B for baroque or G for German. If yours has no letter, play the simple F, 0 1 2 3 4 covered and the rest open, and compare it with an F on a piano or a tuner app. If it sounds right, your recorder has German fingering. On a baroque recorder that fingering sounds out of tune, and you use the “forked” F shown in chapter 6.

00:04:10.288 --> 00:04:18.425
Tip: If you are buying a recorder, choose baroque fingering. It is the system used by most teachers and books, including this one.

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:20.657
Practice.

00:04:21.457 --> 00:04:27.925
First: take your recorder apart and put it back together. Twist the joints gently; never force them.

00:04:28.725 --> 00:04:35.070
Second: line up the holes on the front with the window, and turn the foot joint so its hole is slightly to the right.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:39.158
Third: look for a B or a G near the thumb hole.

00:04:39.958 --> 00:04:46.476
Fourth: without playing, place your fingers over the holes, left hand on top, and feel where each hole is.

00:04:48.276 --> 00:04:51.990
Posture and hands. Holding the recorder the right way.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:05:01.340
A good position makes everything easier: your fingers cover the holes without effort, your breath flows freely, and you can play for longer without getting tired.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:03.594
Your body.

00:05:04.394 --> 00:05:20.091
Sit or stand tall, with your shoulders relaxed and your head straight, as if you were looking at something in front of you. If you sit, keep both feet on the floor and do not lean against the back of the chair. Bring the recorder to your mouth; do not bend your head down to the recorder.

00:05:20.891 --> 00:05:27.506
Hold the recorder at an angle, pointing down and forward, with your elbows relaxed and slightly away from your body.

00:05:29.006 --> 00:05:30.392
Left hand on top.

00:05:31.192 --> 00:05:34.672
The left hand always goes on top, nearest your mouth:

00:05:35.472 --> 00:05:39.149
The left thumb covers the thumb hole on the back (hole 0).

00:05:39.949 --> 00:05:45.137
The index, middle and ring fingers of the left hand cover holes 1, 2 and 3.

00:05:45.937 --> 00:05:50.547
The left little finger is not used. Let it rest above the recorder.

00:05:52.047 --> 00:05:53.280
Right hand below.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:06:00.702
The index, middle, ring and little fingers of the right hand cover holes 4, 5, 6 and 7.

00:06:01.502 --> 00:06:09.559
The right thumb goes behind the recorder, roughly behind holes 4 and 5. It does not cover a hole: it holds the instrument up.

00:06:10.359 --> 00:06:17.891
The recorder rests on three points: your lips, your right thumb and your left thumb. That leaves your fingers free to move.

00:06:19.391 --> 00:06:20.569
Covering the holes.

00:06:21.369 --> 00:06:39.708
Use the soft pads of your fingers, not the tips. Fingers should be gently curved and relaxed, and press just enough to seal the hole. A hole that is not fully covered makes squeaks or wrong notes. To check, look at your fingers afterwards: you should see a light circle on each pad where it covered a hole.

00:06:40.508 --> 00:06:48.403
Keep the fingers you are not using close to their holes, hovering just above them. Fingers that fly far away take longer to come back.

00:06:49.203 --> 00:06:57.973
Tip: Most beginners squeeze too hard. If your hands feel tense or tired, stop, shake them out, and start again with a lighter touch.

00:06:59.473 --> 00:07:00.205
Practice.

00:07:01.005 --> 00:07:07.708
First: stand in front of a mirror with your recorder. Check that your head is straight and your shoulders relaxed.

00:07:08.508 --> 00:07:13.741
Second: put your left hand on top and your right hand below, with the right thumb behind.

00:07:14.541 --> 00:07:20.850
Third: cover all the holes, then lift and lower each finger in turn, slowly, without moving the others.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:26.455
Fourth: look at your finger pads for the circles that show each hole was fully covered.

00:07:28.255 --> 00:07:29.504
That's all for today.

00:07:30.304 --> 00:07:31.695
If you want to keep going:

00:07:32.495 --> 00:07:43.315
FlautaQuest is the app behind the ebook. It teaches the soprano recorder from zero, in short, step-by-step lessons, in the style of language-learning apps like Duolingo.

00:07:44.115 --> 00:07:47.021
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to go with it:

00:07:47.821 --> 00:07:50.056
holding the recorder and breathing well.

00:07:50.856 --> 00:07:52.746
the fingerings of each new note.

00:07:53.546 --> 00:07:55.133
reading simple music.

00:07:55.933 --> 00:07:58.880
learning your first songs, one step at a time.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.762
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who wants to start playing the recorder.

00:08:05.562 --> 00:08:10.397
This episode of Play the Recorder from Zero was narrated with a synthetic voice.

